<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Władysław I Łokietek</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1261–1333</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Władysław the Elbow-high, Wladyslaw I the Elbow-high</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Władysław I Łokietek, 'the Elbow-high', was a Piast duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuyavia">Kuyavia</a> who spent decades fighting rival dukes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> for the fragmented Polish lands. He held smaller duchies such as Brześć Kujawski, Łęczyca and Sieradz on his way to power, and in 1320 was crowned King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, ending almost two centuries of division. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iii-the-great">Casimir III the Great</a> built on his work.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-i-lokietek.jpg" alt="Władysław I Łokietek" width="320" height="533" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bornholm · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kr%C3%B3l_W%C5%82adys%C5%82aw_%C5%81okietek_nagrobek.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
